\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {III}{\ignorespaces Bayesian comparison with parameterized GR-contamination degradation. SSFSR = standard single-field slow-roll inflation. All Bayes factors are closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) evaluations at the central mock detection $\hat  f_{\rm  NL}=-35/8$ with the GR-contamination nuisance added in quadrature to the SPHEREx baseline, $\sigma _{\rm  eff}=\sqrt  {0.7^2+\sigma _{\rm  GR}^2}$; $P(\text  {BF}>3)$ is the fraction of $2\times 10^5$ mock-detection realizations $\hat  f_{\rm  NL}\sim \mathcal  {N}(-35/8,\sigma _{\rm  eff})$ exceeding BF~$=3$ against SSFSR (script and output released with the paper's code, \texttt  {c9g\_bf\_table\_recompute.py}; see Data and Code Availability). The ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column reports the Bayes factor against a tuned multifield competitor with \emph  {narrow} prior $[-5,+5]$ at the delta bounce prior; the no-GR cell (7.0) coincides with the narrow$\to $broad sweep BF~$\sim 7\to 17$ at the delta bounce prior reported in the prose of \S  \ref {sec:bayesian}, and the corresponding broad $[-15,+15]$ competitor gives BF~$\sim 14$ at $\sigma _{\rm  GR}=0.5$. [Correction note: an earlier version of this table quoted BF values ($3.3\times 10^6$/10.9, $4.1\times 10^4$/9.4, 329/7.9) that could not be reproduced from the paper's documented $\sigma (f_{\rm  NL})=0.7$ baseline under any tested mechanism; they are replaced here by the fully documented closed-form computation.] The bounce-vs-inflation comparison survives all treatment scenarios. $^a$The ``Corrected (10\% residual)'' row is, by construction, equal to ``Ideal'' at this template-overlap order: a 10\% residual GR contamination after correction has negligible impact on the Bayes factor at the reported significant-figure level ($\Delta \text  {BF} < 0.1$). It is included as a verification row, not as an independent configuration, and the two rows are not independent scenarios but rather bookend the same GR-free regime.}}{15}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:gr}{{III}{15}{Bayesian comparison with parameterized GR-contamination degradation. SSFSR = standard single-field slow-roll inflation. All Bayes factors are closed-form Eq.~(\ref {eq:bf}) evaluations at the central mock detection $\hat f_{\rm NL}=-35/8$ with the GR-contamination nuisance added in quadrature to the SPHEREx baseline, $\sigma _{\rm eff}=\sqrt {0.7^2+\sigma _{\rm GR}^2}$; $P(\text {BF}>3)$ is the fraction of $2\times 10^5$ mock-detection realizations $\hat f_{\rm NL}\sim \mathcal {N}(-35/8,\sigma _{\rm eff})$ exceeding BF~$=3$ against SSFSR (script and output released with the paper's code, \texttt {c9g\_bf\_table\_recompute.py}; see Data and Code Availability). The ``BF vs.\ Tuned'' column reports the Bayes factor against a tuned multifield competitor with \emph {narrow} prior $[-5,+5]$ at the delta bounce prior; the no-GR cell (7.0) coincides with the narrow$\to $broad sweep BF~$\sim 7\to 17$ at the delta bounce prior reported in the prose of \S \ref {sec:bayesian}, and the corresponding broad $[-15,+15]$ competitor gives BF~$\sim 14$ at $\sigma _{\rm GR}=0.5$. [Correction note: an earlier version of this table quoted BF values ($3.3\times 10^6$/10.9, $4.1\times 10^4$/9.4, 329/7.9) that could not be reproduced from the paper's documented $\sigma (\fnl )=0.7$ baseline under any tested mechanism; they are replaced here by the fully documented closed-form computation.] The bounce-vs-inflation comparison survives all treatment scenarios. $^a$The ``Corrected (10\% residual)'' row is, by construction, equal to ``Ideal'' at this template-overlap order: a 10\% residual GR contamination after correction has negligible impact on the Bayes factor at the reported significant-figure level ($\Delta \text {BF} < 0.1$). It is included as a verification row, not as an independent configuration, and the two rows are not independent scenarios but rather bookend the same GR-free regime}{table.3}{}}
